# Code Intelligence

Use these project-aware code-intelligence tools when they are available. Do not create or modify project instruction files such as `CLAUDE.md`, `CLAUDE.local.md`, or `AGENTS.md` unless the user explicitly asks.

## Serena
- If the project has `.serena/project.yml`, activate the current project with the Serena MCP `activate_project` tool before symbol-level exploration or edits.
- Prefer Serena for exact symbol lookup, references, and surgical symbol edits.
- If Serena is unavailable, continue with normal file reads and mention the gap only when it materially affects confidence.

## GitNexus
- If the project has a GitNexus index (`.gitnexus/`) or GitNexus MCP resources, use GitNexus for architecture discovery, execution flows, blast radius, and pre-commit scope checks.
- Before modifying a function, class, or method in an indexed project, run GitNexus impact analysis for that symbol when the tool is available. If the result is HIGH or CRITICAL risk, warn before editing.
- Before committing in an indexed project, run GitNexus change detection when available.
- If GitNexus reports a stale index, run `gitnexus analyze --index-only` from the project root before relying on it.

## Graphify
- If `graphify-out/GRAPH_REPORT.md` exists in the project root, read it before broad architecture/codebase questions.
- If `graphify-out/wiki/index.md` exists, prefer the wiki for navigation before raw file search.
- For cross-module or code-plus-docs questions, prefer `graphify query`, `graphify path`, or `graphify explain` when the graph exists.
- After code changes in a project with `graphify-out/graph.json`, run `graphify update .` when practical to keep the local graph fresh.

## Tool Choice
- Broad understanding: Graphify first, then GitNexus for execution flows.
- Blast radius and refactors: GitNexus first.
- Exact symbol reads/edits: Serena first.
- Project-specific `CLAUDE.md` instructions override this global fallback when they are more specific.

# Browser and Web Navigation

Three browser tools serve three targets. Pick by where the page lives.

- **`kitesurf` MCP — the public web.** Cloudflare's remote headless browser for agents. It runs
  on Cloudflare Workers, so this machine installs no browser and the request leaves from
  Cloudflare. Use it to open a public URL, read a page, fill a form, click an element, take a
  screenshot, or scrape. It reads `CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID` and `CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN` from the
  environment. If it fails to authenticate, report the missing variable and stop.
  Docs: https://developers.cloudflare.com/browser-run/kitesurf/
- **`obscura` MCP — this machine's network.** A local headless browser written in Rust, with no
  Chrome. Use it for a `localhost` dev server, a private address, or any page kitesurf cannot
  reach from Cloudflare. `obscura serve --port 9222` also exposes a CDP endpoint that Playwright
  and Puppeteer connect to. Docs: https://github.com/h4ckf0r0day/obscura
- **`agent-browser` skill — a real browser.** Use it when the task needs the user's logged-in
  profile, an Electron application, or QA evidence. The `/qa-testing` and `/qa-test-plan` skills
  record screenshots and video through it, so QA stays there.

Prefer `kitesurf` for a public page. It costs this machine nothing and starts fastest.

# UI Quality

The user has no designer, so you are the designer. A screen that works but looks raw is not
done. Deliver the polished version first. Do not wait for the user to ask.

- Before you write UI code, read `~/.claude/skills/_shared/ui-quality.md`. This applies even
  without a plan.
- Build every state: loading, empty, error, pending action and success. Also cover mobile at
  390 px and accessibility.
- Before you hand off, run the visual pass in section 11 of that file. Take screenshots at
  desktop width and at 390 px, critique them, and fix what is weak.
- When a design canvas exists for the change, the `qa-ui` agent compares the screens with it
  before the PR. This applies also when you orchestrate agents without a planning skill. The
  visual pass of the builder does not replace it, and `/ship-it` refuses to open without it.
- A mockup, canvas, artboard or layout of a screen always goes through the `ui-designer`
  agent, also when the user asks for it outside a plan. Do not draw it yourself. The agent
  starts from the real screens: it reads their code and components, and screenshots them
  when the app runs. A running app is not required: without one, it works from the code.

# Agent Models

Subagents run on Sonnet by default (`CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL`). A `model` argument on the
`Agent` tool overrides that default, so set it with care.

- Pass `model: "opus"` only to an agent that writes or reviews a plan, a design or an
  architecture decision.
- Never pass `model: "opus"` to an agent that writes code, fixes code, rebases, runs QA,
  reviews code or reads data. Omit `model` for these agents.
- Codex follows the same split. Code, fixes, QA and code review run on `gpt-6.1-terra`:
  pass `-m gpt-6.1-terra` to `codex exec` and `--model gpt-6.1-terra` to an Orca Codex worker.
  Use `gpt-6.1-sol` only for plans, designs and brainstorming.
- Give an agent the parts of a long file that it needs, not the whole file.
- Each new agent writes its whole starting context to the cache, and every turn reads it
  again. Start fewer agents: give one agent a batch of small related tasks (several review
  fixes, several checks) instead of one agent per task.
- Send a follow-up or a fix to the agent that already holds the context: `SendMessage` to a
  subagent, `orca orchestration send` to an Orca worker. Start a new agent only when the work
  needs a clean context or a different model.

# Pull Requests

Open a pull request only with `/ship-it` (the `pr-description` skill). Never run `gh pr create` by
hand. This applies to every agent and every workflow. `/ship-it` refuses to open a PR before the
build chain ran: a code review at HEAD, QA for a changed flow or screen, and `qa-ui` parity
when a design canvas exists.

# Agents in Orca

When `orca worktree current` resolves, start each agent that builds, fixes, runs QA or records
a demo as an Orca worker, as `~/.claude/skills/_shared/orca-workers.md` describes. Use the
`Agent` tool there only for read-only research and exploration. Outside Orca, use the `Agent`
tool.

# Test Runs — CI First

Several agent sessions share this machine. A full suite with default parallelism can fill
memory and swap and stall every other application. CI is the place for test suites.

- Leave full suites, end-to-end runs, lint and type checks of the whole project to CI. Push
  the branch and read the result with `gh pr checks` or `gh run watch`. The `/ship-it` watch fixes
  a red check. Run a full suite locally only when the user asks for it.
- Locally, run only the focused tests of your change: the failing test and the passing test
  of the TDD cycle, and the test files of the code you changed.
- On this Mac, run focused tests through `~/.local/bin/test-local` with pytest, jest, vitest, or playwright from the package directory.
  The command runs serially in one of two slots that all local sessions share. When both slots are busy, it waits in a queue for up to 9 minutes. Give the Bash call a timeout of 600000 ms, or run it in the background. Do not write a retry loop: exit 75 means the queue stayed full, so do other work and try once more later.
  Do not bypass it with direct commands, containers, package scripts, or delegated workers.
  Do not use watch or UI mode.
- Reuse the test database. Pass `--create-db` only when a migration changed.
- Run containers you start for a test with `--rm`, and stop them when the run ends.

